Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> writes:
> Canageek wrote:
> > [ portable saves and bones via compressed XML ]
> > It could be encrypted to prevent editing of it. Then you could
> > deliberately not encrypt wizard saves to assist with debugging.
> 
> That's indeed a point *not* to use XML that I haven't thought about.
> XML, compressed or not, would open a new dimension in save-scumming.
> Introducing encryption just because of that deficiency is overkill.

On a single-user system, if the player wants to save-scum, or
save-edit, or make the top 10000 entries in the high-score list be
1000-turn ascensions with a score of MAXINT, who are you to tell him
no? It's his computer and his software; he can use it however he
likes. It's arrogant and ultimately futile to try to pretend otherwise
under any circumstances, to say nothing of a case where the "enemy"
can edit the source and rebuild the app at will. (See also: copy
protection, so-called DRM and any other cryptosystem where the
receiver is also the attacker.)

On a multi-user system, the filesystem permissions already
authoritatively prevent that sort of shenanigans. (On a multi-user
system with stupid file permissions, you've bigger problems than
people playing silly buggers with the nethack playground. Likewise, if
a user has root privileges and is willing to abuse them to cheat at a
video game, they're likely willing to abuse said privileges in any
number of more significant ways, too.)

Encrypting saves (where the game engine can programmatically encrypt
and decrypt as needed, but the user cannot) is _far_ harder to do
right than permissions-based protection. If the crypto key is visible
within the user's address space for even an instant, you lose. (Did
you remember to disable swap? Stop coredumps on signals? Prevent them
attaching with a debugger?)

If the answer is "no, but I only want to keep out the stupid people"
then what you have is not encryption, but obfuscation -- and that is a
total, utter, complete waste of bytes and cycles. Actually, it's worse
than useless, as it also creates extra work for people trying to do
non-abusive things that involve the saves and bones.

You're arguing about how to build a deck of cards that slaps your hand
if you try to cheat at solitaire, and it sounds an awful lot like
you're proposing using security through obscurity to do it.

